 "final_text": "I have successfully implemented the `qm::ThreadPool` class as declared in `include/thread_pool.hpp`. Here's a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n**Files Modified:**\n1. `include/thread_pool.hpp` - Added complete implementation with private members and template methods\n2. `src/thread_pool.cpp` - Contains only the non-template methods (constructor and destructor)\n\n## Key Features Implemented:\n\n1. **ThreadPool Constructor/Deconstructor**: \n   - Creates specified number of worker threads\n   - Properly shuts down all threads in destructor, running all queued tasks\n\n2. **submit() Method**:\n   - Schedules callable tasks with arguments\n   - Returns std::future for the result\n   - Supports move-only arguments and callables\n   - Throws exception when submitting to stopped pool\n\n3. **wait() Method**:\n   - Blocks until future is ready\n   - Executes queued tasks while waiting to prevent deadlocks\n   - Works correctly even with single worker thread\n\n4. **parallel_for() Method**:\n   - Distributes work across all worker threads\n   - Blocks until all work is complete\n   - Properly rethrows first exception encountered\n   - Maintains no-deadlock guarantee\n\n## Thread Safety:\n- Fully thread-safe implementation\n- Uses mutexes and condition variables for synchronization\n- Compatible with ThreadSanitizer as required\n- Properly handles concurrent access to shared resources\n\n## Build & Testing:\n- Successfully compiles with C++20 standard\n- All tests pass including deadlock prevention and exception handling\n- Verified with ThreadSanitizer to ensure thread safety\n\nThe implementation maintains all documented guarantees and public signatures while adding necessary private members and helper methods. The code is minimal, efficient, and follows standard C++ practices.",
